  "query_preview": "..High pressure still remains to our east and is in a \nslightly weakened state, due to weak troughing over the western \nGreat Basin. Moisture levels are still on the high side, largely \ndue to influx that arrived on Tuesday. There is also a weak MCV \nalong the Lower Colorado River, forecast to move toward the Kaibab \nPlateau later this afternoon. All these things point toward at \nleast scattered activity again today. The catch is, southerly \nsteering flow and the MCV enhancement will favor areas along and \nnorth of the I-40 corridor, as well as the usual elevated heat \nsource of the White Mountains.\n\nAfter today, the high begins to move westward and strengthen, This \nslowly introduces drier, mid-level air and subsidence, activity will \nslow down. We don't expect a total stop, but it will be slow through \nthe weekend. The high may return to a more favorable location by \nnext week, with easterly steering flow returning, but much can \nchange in a week and we will see.\n\nAdditionally, once the high relocates and strengthens, along with \nthe Monsoon pause/slow down, temperatures will soar. We expect many \nareas to reach the warmest readings so far this year. The various \nheat products that have been issued remain appropriate.",
